<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.20.xsd">

    <!--
      Phase 35-03. The authority for HR configuration.

      D-35-05 makes departments, designations, shifts, leave types, salary components and the tax
      table tenant-managed screens. The permission catalog had no hr.config.* code at all, so those
      screens would have been either unreachable or unguarded.

      TWO CODES, NOT ONE.
      Every dropdown in this phase reads one of these lists, so anyone who can fill an employee form
      must be able to READ the department list — a manager creating an employee needs the options.
      Editing the tax table must not follow from that. Collapsed into a single code, the choice
      would be between a manager who cannot use a dropdown and a manager who can rewrite the tax
      slabs, and both are wrong.

      WHY NO PER-TENANT BACKFILL BLOCK.
      `permissions` and `role_permissions` are GLOBAL tables — role_permissions is
      (role_code, permission_code) with no tenant_id, and a JWT's permission union is computed by
      RolePermissionRepository#findPermissionCodesByRoleCodes over role CODES. Measured, not
      assumed: 13-07-SUMMARY established that `permissions` and `role_permissions` are global and
      that `roles` is the tenant-scoped one. So a grant to OWNER reaches the owner of every tenant
      that already exists the moment this changeset applies. There is nothing to backfill, and the
      prohibition against a grant that only reaches future tenants is satisfied by construction
      rather than by an extra statement.

      Operational note: a user holding an ALREADY-ISSUED access token does not gain the permission
      until their next login or token refresh, because the permission union is baked into the JWT
      at issue time. That is existing behaviour for every permission, not something new here.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-046-hr-config-permissions" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">

        <insert tableName="permissions">
            <column name="code" value="hr.config.view"/>
            <column name="module" value="hr"/>
            <column name="description" value="Read HR configuration lists — departments, designations, shifts, leave types, salary components, tax configuration"/>
        </insert>
        <insert tableName="permissions">
            <column name="code" value="hr.config.manage"/>
            <column name="module" value="hr"/>
            <column name="description" value="Create and edit HR configuration, including the income tax table"/>
        </insert>

        <!--
          VIEW: derived from who already holds hr.employee.view, never enumerated.

          Anyone trusted to see an employee record is trusted to see the list of departments that
          record can point at. Deriving it means a role granted hr.employee.view by a LATER
          changeset (a drift repair, a new role) picks the config-read up automatically, instead of
          silently acquiring a form whose dropdowns 403. This is the same "derived, never
          enumerated" rule RoleCeiling and RoleCatalogService already follow.
        -->
        <sql>
            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code)
            SELECT DISTINCT rp.role_code, 'hr.config.view'
            FROM role_permissions rp
            WHERE rp.permission_code = 'hr.employee.view'
            AND NOT EXISTS (
                SELECT 1 FROM role_permissions existing
                WHERE existing.role_code = rp.role_code
                AND existing.permission_code = 'hr.config.view'
            );
        </sql>

        <!--
          MANAGE: the two tenant-level roles and nobody else.

          Deliberately NOT derived from hr.employee.manage — a branch MANAGER holds that, and a
          branch manager editing the tax table is a money defect. The tax table determines what
          every employee is paid, so the blast radius of one wrong row is every payslip in the
          tenant for a year. Enumerated precisely because the set must not grow by accident.
        -->
        <sql>
            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code)
            SELECT r.role_code, p.permission_code
            FROM (VALUES ('OWNER'), ('TENANT_ADMIN')) AS r(role_code)
            CROSS JOIN (VALUES ('hr.config.view'), ('hr.config.manage')) AS p(permission_code)
            WHERE NOT EXISTS (
                SELECT 1 FROM role_permissions existing
                WHERE existing.role_code = r.role_code
                AND existing.permission_code = p.permission_code
            );
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code IN ('hr.config.view', 'hr.config.manage');</sql>
            <sql>DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code IN ('hr.config.view', 'hr.config.manage');</sql>
        </rollback>

    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
